  "limitations": [
    "P02 remains in progress. No full ISA, physical reference parity, userspace, glibc/systemd, long-running stress or release qualification is claimed.",
    "Run 16 is a failed incremental gate: one Swift test method records 11 issues, and XCTest separately crashes. The later clean pass does not erase either failure.",
    "Run 16 is bound to its exact base-plus-overlay snapshot, not claimed identical to a committed source tree. Three tracked inputs differ from the comparison commit.",
    "Run 17 reports 637 Swift tests: 636 passed and 1 physical-reference test disabled. Run 18 reports 644: 643 passed and 1 disabled. Both XCTest gates complete 10 passes and 1 optional PVH boot skip.",
    "Historical successful totals 580/590/605/612 in reviews through 13/15 were labeled as passes but include one disabled physical-reference test. The additive correction gives actual passes 579/589/604/611 and preserves all earlier receipts.",
    "The clean-run result is consistent with stale incremental object layout contributing to the XCTest crash, but source/test differences also exist. This is an inference, not a controlled proof of the crash root cause.",
    "The arm64 macOS 27 beta / Xcode 26.6 / SDK 26.5 tuple is experimental. Schema validity and optimized executable construction do not imply release qualification.",
    "The source 17 optimized runner is bound to ac0a5aaa7; it does not include source 18 memory changes. Any retained guest probe must use its own exact implementation identity.",
    "Generated FFI inputs and diagnostic executables are separately hash-bound without vendoring binary payloads.",
    "Read-validation tests, typed allocation errors, exact logical bounds and ARC ownership checks do not claim OS resident-memory accounting or direct syscall-level munmap verification. Legacy ByteArray fixture allocation remains subject to Swift Array exhaustion.",
    "Probe 5 uses source 17 and exits1 with an escaping page-fault error after the kernel announces /init. It supplies no userspace workload receipt or ACPI poweroff. Cached state is only the last completed quantum; no exact fault-state or causal diagnosis is claimed."
  ]
